# Recovered payloads must round-trip byte-for-byte.
#
# The Lua, C#, Java, config-table and Unity trees are machine-generated
# decompiler output, not hand-written text. Letting git apply its text/eol
# heuristics could rewrite line endings - or strip CR bytes out of a file whose
# bytes git misjudges - which would silently diverge from the APK the tree was
# decoded from. `-text` disables that class of corruption for every path.
* -text

# The vendored Lua bytecode decompiler is a binary zip, and a zip's integrity
# depends on byte offsets, so it must never be text-normalised. This is spelled
# out rather than left to a path glob: an earlier version of this file used
# `/tools/** text eol=lf`, which silently marked this jar as text and would have
# stripped its 4,293 CR bytes the next time anybody ran `git add` on it.
tools/unluac-batch/unluac.jar binary

# The hand-written toolchain is the opposite case: real text that wants LF on
# every platform. These four extensions do not occur anywhere under
# source-app/, so none of these rules can reach the recovered payload -
# tools/ci-checks.sh asserts that.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf